\DOC W64_GEN_TAC

\TYPE {W64_GEN_TAC : term -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Strips the outermost universal quantifier of a goal, when it ranges over a
64-bit word, instantiating it as {word w} for a fresh numeric variable {w}
supplied by the caller, and adds range assumptions about {w}.

\DESCRIBE
The argument {w : term} is the numeric variable name to use for the newly
introduced quantified value. Applied to a goal whose conclusion is
{!x1 ... xn. t} with {x1} of word type, the leading quantified word variable
{x1} is replaced throughout by {word w}, and every resulting occurrence of
{val (word w)} is collapsed to {w}; other occurrences of {word w} (e.g. as a
register value or inside an equation between words) are left as {word w}. The
remaining quantifiers {x2 ... xn} may be absent.
{
      A ?- !x1 x2 ... xn. t
    ================================================================  W64_GEN_TAC w
      A, val (word w) = w, w < 2 EXP 64 ?- !x2 ... xn. t'
}
\noindent where {t'} is {t} after the substitution and simplification described
above.

\COMMENTS
The observed traces show only this one kind of transformation, applied to goals
whose leading quantifier is over a 64-bit word in machine-code {ensures}
specifications.

\ENDDOC
